{"bill":{"id":"119-hjres-79","congress":119,"billType":"hjres","billNumber":"79","title":"Providing for congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"Review of Final Rule Reclassification of Major Sources as Area Sources Under Section 112 of the Clean Air Act\".","introducedDate":"2025-03-24","latestActionDate":"2025-03-27","latestActionText":"Sponsor introductory remarks on measure. (CR H1328-1329)","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"F000482","cosponsorBioguideIds":["B001306","A000372","B000668"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution nullifies the Environmental Protection Agency final rule titled Review of Final Rule Reclassification of Major Sources as Area Sources Under Section 112 of the Clean Air Act (89 Fed. Reg. 73293) and published on September 10, 2024. Among other elements, the rule requires sources of persistent and bioaccumulative hazardous air pollutants to continue to comply with certain major source emission standards under the Clean Air Act even if the sources reclassify as area sources.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/79","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hjres/BILLSTATUS-119hjres79.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:12.790Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hjres-79","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would nullify an Environmental Protection Agency rule published in September 2024. The EPA rule requires facilities that emit certain persistent and bioaccumulative hazardous air pollutants to continue meeting stricter emission standards even if they reduce their emissions enough to be reclassified from \"major sources\" to \"area sources\" under the Clean Air Act. If this resolution passes, the EPA rule would be blocked and facilities could avoid the stricter standards after reclassification.","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Chemical and industrial manufacturers are commonly subject to hazardous air pollutant standards and would be directly affected by whether reclassified sources must continue compliance."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Refineries and fossil-fuel facilities often emit hazardous air pollutants and face regulatory obligations under Clean Air Act major source standards."},{"industry":"mining","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"Mining and metals processing operations may emit hazardous air pollutants and could be affected by major source reclassification rules."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"4f2c15af68ebd23930aba46b","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:50:17.575Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This joint resolution would cancel the EPA final rule titled Review of Final Rule Reclassification of Major Sources as Area Sources Under Section 112 of the Clean Air Act (89 Fed. Reg.","whoItTouches":"People living near whatever is being regulated.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in the air, water and land where you live. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and the full text of it is further down this page.","titleInPlainWords":null},"overlaps":[{"billId":"119-hjres-79","bioguideId":"F000482","cycle":2026,"score":0.018752224043917395,"matches":[{"industry":"energy-fossil","donorAmount":59500,"donorShare":0.040472648431142406,"billConfidence":0.75,"contribution":0.013797493783344002},{"industry":"manufacturing","donorAmount":12500,"donorShare":0.008502657233433278,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.003285117567462857},{"industry":"mining","donorAmount":9000,"donorShare":0.00612191320807196,"billConfidence":0.6,"contribution":0.0016696126